.TH CJSON_BOOL_VAL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_bool_val, cjson_int_val, cjson_double_val, cjson_str_val \- read a scalar value from a DOM node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

bool        cjson_bool_val(cjson node);
long long   cjson_int_val(cjson node);
double      cjson_double_val(cjson node);
const char *cjson_str_val(cjson node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Each function returns the value that node holds.  It calls
_ccol_fatal_err() when node is NULL, and also when node is not of the
type that the function reads: CJSON_BOOL for cjson_bool_val(),
CJSON_INTEGER for cjson_int_val(), CJSON_FLOAT for cjson_double_val()
and CJSON_STRING for cjson_str_val().

cjson_str_val() returns a pointer to the node's own copy of the
string, and the caller must not free it.  The pointer is borrowed and
stays valid until the node gets a new value or is destroyed, so any
of these ends it: a cjson_set(3) that reaches the node, a
cjson_list_remove(3), cjson_dictionary_remove(3) or cjson_delete(3)
of the node, a cjson_dictionary_set(3) that replaces it, and a
destroy of its tree.  Copy the text if you need it longer.  The
string is always well-formed UTF-8 whatever built the tree, because
the parser refuses a document whose strings are not UTF-8, and
cjson_create_string(3) and cjson_set(3) refuse such a string from
the caller.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  A node of the type that the function reads.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
cjson_bool_val() returns the bool, cjson_int_val() the long long and
cjson_double_val() the double.  cjson_str_val() returns a pointer to
the string inside the node, borrowed as described above.  When node
is NULL or of the wrong type, none of the functions returns.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_type (3),
.BR cjson_create_string (3),
.BR cjson_parse (3)
